        "id": 1259838
      },
      "writtenOn": "2019-10-15T13:41:06Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "why do we need this? so we don\u0027t get truncation? I would rather we get a compiler error when you try to cross these two enums",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 26,
        "startChar": 1,
        "endLine": 27,
        "endChar": 31
      },
      "revId": "2e4969f10cf5288781874a11baa1bcdbd7f87bda",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "3cd96ae5_edcc4237",
        "filename": "include/gpio_signal.h",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 27,
      "author": {
        "id": 1212794
      },
      "writtenOn": "2019-10-15T19:17:36Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Yes, a compiler error when mixing up the enums would be ideal, but isn\u0027t possible in C AFAIK.\n\nThe reason for adding GPIO_LIMIT is to ensure that sizeof(enum gpio_signal) \u003d\u003d sizeof(enum ioex_signal). Without it, then (on NPCX) sizeof(enum gpio_signal) \u003d 1, sizeof(enum ioex_signal) \u003d 2. Then if you do something like this:\n\n  enum ioex_signal foo \u003d IOEX_EN_USB_A0_5V;\n  gpio_set_level(foo, 1);\n\nthen there is no compiler error, foo gets silently truncated, the wrong GPIO gets set, and an ASSERT() in gpio_set_level() can\u0027t catch the error at run-time.",
      "parentUuid": "3f951813_e08728a2",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 26,
        "startChar": 1,
        "endLine": 27,
        "endChar": 31
      },
      "revId": "2e4969f10cf5288781874a11baa1bcdbd7f87bda",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "0ec94c91_03ec841e",
        "filename": "include/gpio_signal.h",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 27,
      "author": {
        "id": 1212794
      },
      "writtenOn": "2019-10-16T05:48:43Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I think -Wenum-conversion might do what we want, which has been in clang for a while, and just got added to GCC a month ago, after a few years of debate!\nhttps://gcc.gnu.org/bugzilla/show_bug.cgi?id\u003d78736#c13",
      "parentUuid": "3cd96ae5_edcc4237",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 26,
        "startChar": 1,
        "endLine": 27,
        "endChar": 31
      },
      "revId": "2e4969f10cf5288781874a11baa1bcdbd7f87bda",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
